//Draft, 2026-10-13. Built from my raw notes at the bottom and from Justin's answers. Mine to rewrite.//

I have never sat in an [[Oka Chair]]. I have only looked at it and talked about it.

In the picture I know best it sits in a pasture with sheep around it. It is low. The arms are wide. The legs are thick, thicker than a chair's legs need to be.

Why?

!! The wood

The frame is city timber, from trees that came down in Baltimore ([[The four Ds]]). Wood like that has knots and worm holes.

Normally you would cut around them. You cut the stock down, avoid the knots and the bug holes, and go looking for more "perfect" wood for the legs and spindles. [[Justin Beitzel]] says most wood waste comes from exactly this. You lose a big piece of the board around each mark.

The Oka Chair does the opposite. Its parts are wide enough that nobody wonders whether a knot is holding the chair up. The flaw stays in.

!! The sitter

The arms are wide for a different reason. Justin wanted a casual platform: a chair you could sling a leg over the side of, or sit cross-legged in to read.

!! The room

There is a third reason. The chair has to compete with other big lounge chairs.

!! What it is showing

The form was chosen so imperfect wood could be used, and then to showcase those imperfections.

A chair made the traditional way is made by a craftsperson, and the goal is to showcase the craft. So you want better stock. You work around the flaws, or you hide them: on the underside of a seat, or as the inside parts of a drawer.

On this chair the flaws are to be shown. The Oka Chair is showcasing where the wood came from. So it wants the knots.

Imperfect stock always had somewhere to go: smaller projects, plain functional furniture, shop tools.

Salvaged wood is the kind that, in some hands and some contexts, would be seen as undesirable. That seems to be changing. I still can't see the makers of a Finn Juhl chair, or of [[Hans Wegner]]'s [[Wishbone Chair]], wanting wood with these qualities.

!! A sign

These choices are still [[Signs Signaling Sustainability]]. The chair is visual communication, and it is conceptual object design. See [[The object is the visible result of a way of working]].

!! What it cost

Smaller chairs that are still comfortable could have been made. Perhaps two for every big Oka Chair. Is that smart? Does that make sense at the price point and statement level?

I don't know yet.

I should go sit in one.

See [[Truth to Materials]], [[We used to know how]], [[Wishbone Chair]], [[Materialshed]], [[Mingei]]

